          "english_quote": "τῶν αὐτοῦ is deliberately broad and may denote his people, household, or possessions; “those who are his” preserves that ambiguity.",
          "issue": "The personal main-text rendering is defensible, and the note already names the possessions alternative. However, its claim that “those who are his” preserves the entire ambiguity is inaccurate: “who” selects persons rather than possessions. The distinction between the main-text choice and the alternative therefore needs more honest description.",
          "suggested_revision": "Revise the note to state that the main text selects his people or household in the Paris allegory, while the Greek can also refer to his possessions. Alternatively, consider “both himself and his own” as a broader main-text candidate. Any amended wording or rationale requires fresh review; the referent ambiguity itself does not require blocking.",
